  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a timeline of how ocean observing tools and coordinated programs have expanded from the 1950s to the 2020s. A large green arrow running through the center marks the decades and emphasizes continued growth in marine measurements over time.\n\nEarly observations are represented by mooring floats and buoys in the 1950s, followed by modern research vessels and rosette/CTD sampling systems in the 1960s. Through the 1970s and 1980s, additional ship-based and profiling technologies appear, including ROVs, Niskin bottles, acoustic Doppler current profilers, and satellites. In the 1990s and 2000s, autonomous platforms become more prominent, especially gliders and Argo floats, including BGC-Argo for biogeochemical measurements. The 2010s and 2020s add newer systems such as Deep Argo and uncrewed surface vehicles.\n\nAbove and below the timeline, bracketed labels link these technologies to major international observing and synthesis efforts, such as GEOSECS, WOCE/JGOFS, CUEA, Argo, GO-SHIP, GEOTRACES, IMBeR, SOLAS, GLOBEC, and SOCAT. Overall, the image shows a shift from relatively sparse, ship-centered sampling toward a diverse, global observing network that combines vessels, fixed instruments, satellites, and autonomous platforms.",
